+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ELLEaKxn51tsD3s853ULPb","type":"tool_result","content":"# Phase 5: Retry with Backoff\n\nAdd automatic retry functionality with exponential backoff.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`fail(taskId, error?)`** - Mark a processing task as failed. Triggers automatic retry if retries remain.\n- **`complete(taskId)`** - Mark a processing task as completed.\n\n## Updated Methods\n\n- **`enqueue(queueName, payload, options?)`** - Options can now include:\n  - `maxRetries`: maximum number of retry attempts (default: 3)\n  - `backoffMs`: base delay for exponential backoff in milliseconds (default: 1000)\n\n## Requirements\n\n- When `fail()` is called and retries remain, the task is re-enqueued with a delay\n- Backoff formula: `backoffMs * 2^(attempt - 1)` where attempt starts at 1\n  - 1st retry: 1000ms, 2nd retry: 2000ms, 3rd retry: 4000ms\n- `task.attempts` tracks how many times the task has been attempted (starts at 1 on first dequeue)\n- Custom `maxRetries` can be set per task\n- Retried tasks preserve their original queue name and priority\n- If a task has exhausted all retries, it remains in `failed` status (see Phase 6 for DLQ)\n# Phase 6: Dead Letter Queue\n\nAdd dead letter queue (DLQ) support for tasks that have exhausted all retries.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`getDLQ(queueName)`** - Return an array of all dead-letter tasks for the specified queue.\n- **`retryDLQ(taskId)`** - Move a dead-letter task back to its original queue for another round of retries. Returns the re-enqueued task.\n- **`clearDLQ(queueName)`** - Remove all dead-letter tasks for the specified queue.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- When a task exhausts all retry attempts (from Phase 5), it automatically moves to the DLQ\n- DLQ tasks have status `'failed'` and are stored separately from the main queue\n- Each DLQ task retains its original error information (`task.lastError`)\n- DLQ tasks track how many times they've failed (`task.failCount`)\n- `retryDLQ(taskId)` resets the task's retry count, giving it a fresh set of retries\n- `retryDLQ(taskId)` places the task back in its original queue with original priority\n- `clearDLQ(queueName)` removes all DLQ entries for that queue\n- DLQ is per-queue (each named queue has its own DLQ)\n# Phase 7: Task Dependencies\n\nAdd support for task dependencies, where tasks can wait for other tasks to complete before becoming available.\n\n## Updated Methods\n\n- **`enqueue(queueName, payload, options?)`** - Options can now include:\n  - `dependsOn`: an array of task IDs that must complete before this task becomes available\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`getDependencies(taskId)`** - Return dependency information for a task, including each dependency's ID and current status.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- A task with dependencies remains blocked (not available for dequeue) until ALL dependencies have `completed` status\n- When a dependency completes (via `complete(taskId)`), the system checks if any dependent tasks are now unblocked\n- Circular dependencies must be detected and throw an error at enqueue time\n  - Direct circular: A depends on B, B depends on A\n  - Indirect circular: A → B → C → A\n- `getDependencies(taskId)` returns an array of `{ taskId, status }` objects\n- Dependency on a non-existent task ID should throw an error\n- A failed dependency does NOT auto-fail the dependent task - it stays blocked\n- Cascading dependencies work: if A depends on B and B depends on C, A is only available after both B and C complete\n# Phase 8: Concurrency Control\n\nAdd per-queue concurrency limits to control how many tasks can be processed simultaneously.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`setConcurrency(queueName, limit)`** - Set the maximum number of tasks that can be in `processing` status simultaneously for a queue.\n- **`getConcurrency(queueName)`** - Return concurrency information: `{ current: number, max: number }`.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- `dequeue()` returns `null` if the queue has reached its concurrency limit, even if tasks are available\n- `complete(taskId)` and `fail(taskId)` free up a concurrency slot\n- Default concurrency is unlimited (no limit) until `setConcurrency` is called\n- Each queue has its own independent concurrency limit\n- Concurrency interacts correctly with priorities (highest priority task still chosen, but only if a slot is available)\n- `getConcurrency(queueName)` returns the current number of processing tasks and the configured maximum\n  - For unlimited queues, `max` should be `Infinity` or `0` to indicate no limit","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"73578245-3916-44ab-a99c-085e0622a3f4","uuid":"88d98f7e-c7f7-47fc-93f0-98eb46582b45","timestamp":"2026-07-25T15:49:09.460Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Phase 5: Retry with Backoff\n\nAdd automatic retry functionality with exponential backoff.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`fail(taskId, error?)`** - Mark a processing task as failed. Triggers automatic retry if retries remain.\n- **`complete(taskId)`** - Mark a processing task as completed.\n\n## Updated Methods\n\n- **`enqueue(queueName, payload, options?)`** - Options can now include:\n  - `maxRetries`: maximum number of retry attempts (default: 3)\n  - `backoffMs`: base delay for exponential backoff in milliseconds (default: 1000)\n\n## Requirements\n\n- When `fail()` is called and retries remain, the task is re-enqueued with a delay\n- Backoff formula: `backoffMs * 2^(attempt - 1)` where attempt starts at 1\n  - 1st retry: 1000ms, 2nd retry: 2000ms, 3rd retry: 4000ms\n- `task.attempts` tracks how many times the task has been attempted (starts at 1 on first dequeue)\n- Custom `maxRetries` can be set per task\n- Retried tasks preserve their original queue name and priority\n- If a task has exhausted all retries, it remains in `failed` status (see Phase 6 for DLQ)\n# Phase 6: Dead Letter Queue\n\nAdd dead letter queue (DLQ) support for tasks that have exhausted all retries.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`getDLQ(queueName)`** - Return an array of all dead-letter tasks for the specified queue.\n- **`retryDLQ(taskId)`** - Move a dead-letter task back to its original queue for another round of retries. Returns the re-enqueued task.\n- **`clearDLQ(queueName)`** - Remove all dead-letter tasks for the specified queue.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- When a task exhausts all retry attempts (from Phase 5), it automatically moves to the DLQ\n- DLQ tasks have status `'failed'` and are stored separately from the main queue\n- Each DLQ task retains its original error information (`task.lastError`)\n- DLQ tasks track how many times they've failed (`task.failCount`)\n- `retryDLQ(taskId)` resets the task's retry count, giving it a fresh set of retries\n- `retryDLQ(taskId)` places the task back in its original queue with original priority\n- `clearDLQ(queueName)` removes all DLQ entries for that queue\n- DLQ is per-queue (each named queue has its own DLQ)\n# Phase 7: Task Dependencies\n\nAdd support for task dependencies, where tasks can wait for other tasks to complete before becoming available.\n\n## Updated Methods\n\n- **`enqueue(queueName, payload, options?)`** - Options can now include:\n  - `dependsOn`: an array of task IDs that must complete before this task becomes available\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`getDependencies(taskId)`** - Return dependency information for a task, including each dependency's ID and current status.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- A task with dependencies remains blocked (not available for dequeue) until ALL dependencies have `completed` status\n- When a dependency completes (via `complete(taskId)`), the system checks if any dependent tasks are now unblocked\n- Circular dependencies must be detected and throw an error at enqueue time\n  - Direct circular: A depends on B, B depends on A\n  - Indirect circular: A → B → C → A\n- `getDependencies(taskId)` returns an array of `{ taskId, status }` objects\n- Dependency on a non-existent task ID should throw an error\n- A failed dependency does NOT auto-fail the dependent task - it stays blocked\n- Cascading dependencies work: if A depends on B and B depends on C, A is only available after both B and C complete\n# Phase 8: Concurrency Control\n\nAdd per-queue concurrency limits to control how many tasks can be processed simultaneously.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`setConcurrency(queueName, limit)`** - Set the maximum number of tasks that can be in `processing` status simultaneously for a queue.\n- **`getConcurrency(queueName)`** - Return concurrency information: `{ current: number, max: number }`.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- `dequeue()` returns `null` if the queue has reached its concurrency limit, even if tasks are available\n- `complete(taskId)` and `fail(taskId)` free up a concurrency slot\n- Default concurrency is unlimited (no limit) until `setConcurrency` is called\n- Each queue has its own independent concurrency limit\n- Concurrency interacts correctly with priorities (highest priority task still chosen, but only if a slot is available)\n- `getConcurrency(queueName)` returns the current number of processing tasks and the configured maximum\n  - For unlimited queues, `max` should be `Infinity` or `0` to indicate no limit","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
