+{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0163cSgnq2UfsVyoRjX5zqd9","type":"tool_result","content":"# Phase 9: Progress and Cancellation\n\nAdd progress tracking and task cancellation support.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`updateProgress(taskId, percent, message?)`** - Report progress for a processing task (0-100).\n- **`getProgress(taskId)`** - Return progress info: `{ percent: number, message?: string, updatedAt: Date }`.\n- **`cancel(taskId)`** - Cancel a pending or processing task. Sets status to `'cancelled'`.\n- **`onProgress(taskId, callback)`** - Subscribe to progress updates. Callback receives `{ percent, message, updatedAt }`.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Progress percentage must be between 0 and 100\n- `updateProgress` fires all registered `onProgress` callbacks for that task\n- `cancel()` works on both `pending` and `processing` tasks\n- A cancelled pending task is removed from the queue (not available for dequeue)\n- A cancelled processing task has its status set to `'cancelled'`\n- Cancelled tasks are NOT retried (even if they have retries remaining)\n- `getProgress(taskId)` returns `null` if no progress has been reported yet\n- The `message` parameter in `updateProgress` is optional\n- `onProgress` returns an unsubscribe function\n# Phase 10: Recurring Tasks (Cron)\n\nAdd support for recurring scheduled tasks using simple cron-like expressions.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`schedule(queueName, cronExpression, taskTemplate)`** - Create a recurring task schedule. Returns a schedule object with an `id`.\n- **`unschedule(scheduleId)`** - Remove a schedule, stopping future occurrences.\n- **`listSchedules(queueName?)`** - List all active schedules, optionally filtered by queue name.\n- **`tick()`** - Manually advance and check schedules, triggering any that are due. For testing purposes.\n\n## Cron Expression Subset\n\nSupport a simple subset of cron expressions:\n\n| Expression       | Meaning              |\n|------------------|----------------------|\n| `*/N * * * *`    | Every N minutes      |\n| `0 * * * *`      | Every hour (on the hour) |\n| `0 0 * * *`      | Every day at midnight |\n\n## Requirements\n\n- `schedule()` returns a schedule object: `{ id, queueName, cronExpression, taskTemplate, active: true }`\n- `tick()` checks all active schedules and enqueues tasks for any that are due\n- The `taskTemplate` is used as the payload for enqueued tasks\n- `unschedule(scheduleId)` marks the schedule as inactive\n- `listSchedules()` returns only active schedules\n- Multiple schedules can coexist on the same or different queues\n- Scheduled tasks are regular tasks once enqueued (subject to all other queue rules)\n# Phase 11: Middleware Pipeline\n\nAdd a middleware system for queue processing pipelines.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`use(queueName, middleware)`** - Add a middleware function to a queue's processing pipeline.\n- **`process(queueName, handler)`** - Register a task handler for a queue and begin processing. The handler runs after all middleware.\n\n## Middleware Signature\n\n```typescript\ntype Middleware = (task: Task, next: () => Promise<void>) => Promise<void>;\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Middleware functions run in the order they were added (first added = first to run)\n- Each middleware must call `next()` to pass control to the next middleware (and eventually the handler)\n- If a middleware does NOT call `next()`, the task processing is skipped\n- Middleware can modify the task object (e.g., add metadata)\n- If a middleware throws an error, the task is marked as failed\n- Each queue has its own independent middleware stack\n- Middleware receives the full task context (task object)\n- `process()` dequeues a task and runs it through the middleware pipeline, then the handler\n# Phase 12: Graceful Shutdown\n\nAdd graceful shutdown capabilities to the task queue.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`shutdown(timeout?)`** - Initiate graceful shutdown. Returns a Promise that resolves with a summary.\n  - `timeout`: maximum milliseconds to wait for processing tasks (default: 30000)\n- **`onShutdown(callback)`** - Register a callback to be called during shutdown.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- After `shutdown()` is called, `enqueue()` should throw an error\n- `shutdown()` waits for all currently processing tasks to complete\n- If processing tasks don't complete within the timeout, they are force-cancelled\n- `onShutdown` callbacks are called during the shutdown process\n- The returned summary object includes:\n  - `completed`: number of tasks that completed during shutdown\n  - `cancelled`: number of tasks that were force-cancelled\n  - `elapsed`: time in milliseconds the shutdown took\n- Multiple calls to `shutdown()` should be idempotent (return the same promise/result)\n- If there are no processing tasks, shutdown resolves immediately\n- Force-cancelled tasks have their status set to `'cancelled'`","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"73578245-3916-44ab-a99c-085e0622a3f4","uuid":"0af28306-c62d-4e5d-9bef-8a706ceb6a5b","timestamp":"2026-07-25T15:49:09.763Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Phase 9: Progress and Cancellation\n\nAdd progress tracking and task cancellation support.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`updateProgress(taskId, percent, message?)`** - Report progress for a processing task (0-100).\n- **`getProgress(taskId)`** - Return progress info: `{ percent: number, message?: string, updatedAt: Date }`.\n- **`cancel(taskId)`** - Cancel a pending or processing task. Sets status to `'cancelled'`.\n- **`onProgress(taskId, callback)`** - Subscribe to progress updates. Callback receives `{ percent, message, updatedAt }`.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Progress percentage must be between 0 and 100\n- `updateProgress` fires all registered `onProgress` callbacks for that task\n- `cancel()` works on both `pending` and `processing` tasks\n- A cancelled pending task is removed from the queue (not available for dequeue)\n- A cancelled processing task has its status set to `'cancelled'`\n- Cancelled tasks are NOT retried (even if they have retries remaining)\n- `getProgress(taskId)` returns `null` if no progress has been reported yet\n- The `message` parameter in `updateProgress` is optional\n- `onProgress` returns an unsubscribe function\n# Phase 10: Recurring Tasks (Cron)\n\nAdd support for recurring scheduled tasks using simple cron-like expressions.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`schedule(queueName, cronExpression, taskTemplate)`** - Create a recurring task schedule. Returns a schedule object with an `id`.\n- **`unschedule(scheduleId)`** - Remove a schedule, stopping future occurrences.\n- **`listSchedules(queueName?)`** - List all active schedules, optionally filtered by queue name.\n- **`tick()`** - Manually advance and check schedules, triggering any that are due. For testing purposes.\n\n## Cron Expression Subset\n\nSupport a simple subset of cron expressions:\n\n| Expression       | Meaning              |\n|------------------|----------------------|\n| `*/N * * * *`    | Every N minutes      |\n| `0 * * * *`      | Every hour (on the hour) |\n| `0 0 * * *`      | Every day at midnight |\n\n## Requirements\n\n- `schedule()` returns a schedule object: `{ id, queueName, cronExpression, taskTemplate, active: true }`\n- `tick()` checks all active schedules and enqueues tasks for any that are due\n- The `taskTemplate` is used as the payload for enqueued tasks\n- `unschedule(scheduleId)` marks the schedule as inactive\n- `listSchedules()` returns only active schedules\n- Multiple schedules can coexist on the same or different queues\n- Scheduled tasks are regular tasks once enqueued (subject to all other queue rules)\n# Phase 11: Middleware Pipeline\n\nAdd a middleware system for queue processing pipelines.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`use(queueName, middleware)`** - Add a middleware function to a queue's processing pipeline.\n- **`process(queueName, handler)`** - Register a task handler for a queue and begin processing. The handler runs after all middleware.\n\n## Middleware Signature\n\n```typescript\ntype Middleware = (task: Task, next: () => Promise<void>) => Promise<void>;\n```\n\n## Requirements\n\n- Middleware functions run in the order they were added (first added = first to run)\n- Each middleware must call `next()` to pass control to the next middleware (and eventually the handler)\n- If a middleware does NOT call `next()`, the task processing is skipped\n- Middleware can modify the task object (e.g., add metadata)\n- If a middleware throws an error, the task is marked as failed\n- Each queue has its own independent middleware stack\n- Middleware receives the full task context (task object)\n- `process()` dequeues a task and runs it through the middleware pipeline, then the handler\n# Phase 12: Graceful Shutdown\n\nAdd graceful shutdown capabilities to the task queue.\n\n## New Methods\n\n- **`shutdown(timeout?)`** - Initiate graceful shutdown. Returns a Promise that resolves with a summary.\n  - `timeout`: maximum milliseconds to wait for processing tasks (default: 30000)\n- **`onShutdown(callback)`** - Register a callback to be called during shutdown.\n\n## Requirements\n\n- After `shutdown()` is called, `enqueue()` should throw an error\n- `shutdown()` waits for all currently processing tasks to complete\n- If processing tasks don't complete within the timeout, they are force-cancelled\n- `onShutdown` callbacks are called during the shutdown process\n- The returned summary object includes:\n  - `completed`: number of tasks that completed during shutdown\n  - `cancelled`: number of tasks that were force-cancelled\n  - `elapsed`: time in milliseconds the shutdown took\n- Multiple calls to `shutdown()` should be idempotent (return the same promise/result)\n- If there are no processing tasks, shutdown resolves immediately\n- Force-cancelled tasks have their status set to `'cancelled'`","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
